zation  so can you tell talk a little bit about  like um how do you  see the potential for um like  cross-collaboration across the school as  kind of nbsa  any thoughts on kind of potential  integration with undergrads  or um kind of by extension what what are  some of the  initiatives or things that you hope nbsa  is going to be able to  um kind of approach in the coming year  yeah um so the mbsa i think was unique  because we were  the last actually grad program to add a  native  uh a native club um so all the other  grad programs  including the undergrad have really  strong native  club communities so what we're trying to  do is actually work with those other  grad  programs to see what has worked for them  um how can we generate community within  the business school  so we've been leaning on them a lot um  to help guide us  get this club off the ground and we hope  in the future that we could create  um like greg mentioned snags as more of  a collective grad unit for all native  um grad students at stanford and  hopefully we can have some events  together and just  continue to enjoy the powwows we weren't  able to participate last year but we  hope we can  participate this year um even with snags  last year  we were able to just go to dinner and  just have fun together and i think just  spending time  with that community was really special  and also just having friends outside of  your grad program to add perspective  and other native perspectives into your  community as well  yeah i'll add to that is that sometimes  the gsb very much can feel  like we're a bubble um there's so much  going on and so much to do within our  own community that  it takes some very concerted effort to  um to expand outside of the business  school  and knack has really provided both renee  and myself with  with that um with that perspective and  that community that's not just at the  gsb  and so continuing to continuing to  liaise with them and then kind of use  use them to help build up what but we're  hoping to be a strong  presence at the gsb that knapp has in  broader stanford community is definitely  um  of importance to the two of us and greg  there's questions  for you um how have you seen like some  of the collaboration among the different  um  organizations on campus like how have  things evolved over time  um very much like renee said  the organizations are working together  closer and closer um  there are you know only about 100 native  grad students  which compared to many schools that's a  whole lot but it's still only 100.  and so we really do need to be together  work together collaborate and  charlotte said you know biscuits really  intense and  you know you're in your own little  business school world and it's sometimes  hard to break out of that  but doing that um you know adds to your  friend network your  social network your professional network  and  a different perspective i mean it's so  important to  like kind of just step away and  relax a little bit out of whatever  program you're in  and will you asked about undergrads the  the native undergrads are really hungry  to learn about graduate school you know  how did you choose it  why did you decide this you know mba  instead of law school  and so they they love to hear from  native grad students so  possible idea for future events kind of  planting the scenes  you know i was trying to build  connections wherever we can  um so i'm actually curious a little bit  more about  um you both mentioned a lot about  introspection  um as part of the business school  journey which i think  when people think business school  introspection is not necessarily  the first thing they think of um  so have either of you so you've taken  interpersonal dynamics can you walk us  through like  what kind of what did you do in those  classes and like  did you have any aha moments or  observations about your  own leadership development  absolutely um and it's true i think that  um  that when you think about an mba program  you're thinking about accounting and  finance which is very much part of the  gsb  um but just like any other program  but um but as soon as you really start  learning about  mbas it's just as much about  the uh the new the numbers and that like  quantitative rigor as it is about  becoming a leader and that and becoming  a leader i think one of the most  important qualities that i that i see  and and leaders i admire and respect is  um is their ability to be self-aware and  to have a growth mindset towards um  their own development opportunities even  at the helm of a large organization  um so self self reflection and that  introspective piece really is  um is integral to to applying and being  a part of an mba program  but to answer your your more specific  question um  that those those aha moments they really  they  began day one because um  leadership labs the the class that renee  spoke to um the both of us now leading  in our mba two-year  that is that is something that you take  fall quarter and  you immediately learn about um  the action impact model for delivering  feedback as an example  and showing that you know instead of  feeling frustrated and saying  you did this or like you you were really  rude to me  um just being able to reframe feedback  and saying you know when you spoke in  this way  i felt disrespected and and that just  slight switch in in the way you use  language um  was a pretty big aha moment for me week  one  um and if i were to  tell you about all of the aha moments  that i've had in the past  year and a half or so it'd be a time for  an entirely  separate panel um but really just  through  through lead labs through interpersonal  dynamics now being a fellow  um it's been amazing just seeing a lot  of things kind of  come together in ways that i never would  have expected  and really um really helped me hone in  on kind of  what what makes me me  yeah and i think building off what  charlie was saying  i think one question i've really  reflected on  or spent a lot of my introspection on is  just why would someone  follow me like what do i bring to the  table  that makes it um unique or how do i  generate buy-in and build trust  and so i think through like  interpersonal dynamics you explore a lot  of these  topics of how do you think about  building trust but then how do you  integrate that with firing someone  how do you think about being your most  authentic self  but being able to adapt to any business  environment  or personal environment and so thinking  through those questions of  who am i what do i bring that's unique  and why would i  why would i invite team members to  follow me  i think it just takes a personal  responsibility and an empathetic leader  to understand what every person is going  through and and what it really truly  means  to ask someone to follow you and that  responsibility you take on as a leader  so  thinking through those questions at the  gsb um beyond  all of the other like leadership  opportunities here has been something  that has been  really beneficial for me it's  interesting it's like  um come urban mba and do some  existential pondering  oh so we are getting close to the top of  the hour this time actually really  flew but um for our last question  i'm gonna claim this one for myself  for our students to a younger you  whether it was in the application  process  or maybe it was last year that first  year as you were starting  what advice would you give yourself and  for greg  what advice would you give to folks who  are thinking about  graduate programs just kind of writ  large  so i'll give you a moment to um think on  that and then whenever someone's ready  go ahead and pop on  i'll start um and also  renee is referring me to me as charlie  because charlie is my nickname and  that's what she calls me i  keep meaning to to call that out um but  yes just in case that was confusing to  anybody  um but anyways uh advice that i would  give  my younger self i think is still advice  that applies  today um but it's to  trust that things will work out  the way that they ought to um  i i make up her i'm intentionally don't  use the word  should because i think that like should  can be a really  um scary path to go down of  what like should versus should not but  um  you know there were there were times in  college and jobs that i didn't get and  career moments in my career that i  really thought um  i really thought we're gonna we're gonna  be damning or just were  were keeping me up at night um  got passed over for promotions a lot of  just a lot of things where where there  was maybe signs of  of darkness or strife that um  that looking back on now i know  that they were they all happened for a  reason which is super  cheesy to say and to hear myself say out  loud to but  um but everything  led me here one way or another um and  i'm really  i'm really happy to be here and i think  that  um it's advice that i need to continue  to continue to take even  um even now as we look ahead  and think about have a lot of choice in  front of us  as far as you know where um where to end  up next year  and how to spend our remaining two  quarters  and i think that it's it's a lesson that  that will be kind of taught and  re-taught time and time again  yeah i think that's amazing advice um  trusting the process and having the  faith that it'll work out  i think if i could go back and tell  myself  something it would just be to believe in  myself  a bit more um which is a beyond that  like trust the process  piece i was so hesitant to apply to  business school at first and i thought  stanford was just such a reach for me i  was like no way  and um i think if i would just go back  and just say believe in myself  and going through that application  process i know it's just so taxing  um and it can take a long time but i  wish i  just was my i know everyone can be their  biggest critic but i wish i was  my biggest fan too um and going through  that process and remembering like  you do enough you are enough um you  deserve  and well everything will work out um so  i think i would  uh tell myself that and give myself some  confidence  um to keep going and pursue whatever  dream i have  to add to all that excellent advice um  develop a strong why on what on  why you want to go to bus school or any  graduate program  um and that's the reflection and where  you're going to go with it what are you  going to do with it  do research i mean this is an investment  of two years of time and a  fair amount of tuition so you wouldn't  just jump into an investment like  hey that sounds great let me do that so  you know  do do research and and find the right  fit um  as renee said i think reach um  you know gosh way back in the day when i  applied to stanford's and undergrads  like oh there's no  and it's like whoa oh my gosh there we  are  and if you don't apply you absolutely  won't get in  um there's a great book uh john maxwell  sometimes you win sometimes you learn  and so if if you if you need to learn  uh you know learn and try to get up and  try it again  great thank you so much  to everybody who joined us today as  in our audience thank you so much to our  panelists for joining us  sharing time and sharing space and also  thank you for allowing me to be part of  the community  you just bring so much joy to like  my work and my week and i hope we're  able to share some of that joy with  everybody out here today

